What: the kqueue Selector treats a read event that reports TCP urgent data (EV_OOBAND, no
  EV_EOF) on a registered socket that has SO_OOBINLINE off and nothing to read (FIONREAD 0) as
  no event: it discards the urgent byte (recv MSG_OOB; EINVAL means it already was), as
  Socket.setOOBInline specifies urgent data is while the option is off, does not select the
  key, and re-registers the socket's read filter with EV_CLEAR (EV_DELETE, then EV_ADD |
  EV_CLEAR), so kqueue reports only new activity on it. The first real read event on such a
  socket (normal data, EOF) re-registers the filter level-triggered before the key is
  selected. When every polled event was ignored, select polls again for what remains of its
  timeout instead of returning early. KQueue gains EV_OOBAND, EV_EOF and a native
  discardUrgentData(fd).
Why on 10.9: 10.9's kqueue keeps reporting such a socket readable (EVFILT_READ, EV_OOBAND,
  data 0) for as long as it sits at the urgent mark with nothing to read, while read() returns
  nothing (EAGAIN). Discarding the byte does not clear that state; only normal data arriving
  does. Without this patch a peer that sends one urgent byte pegs a core in every select() on
  that selector, platform or virtual thread (about 550,000 kevent returns a second in C). With
  it, kevent blocks: in C 2 kevent returns and no CPU in a 1 s wait, then normal data is
  reported at once; in Java select(1000) and an untimed select() use ~0 ms of CPU, and the
  data, a second urgent byte, and EOF are all still reported (LegacySmoke's urgentData check
  measures the thread's CPU across select(1000)). EV_CLEAR lasts only until that first real
  event, so a partial read cannot strand data.
  Not fixed here: 10.9's filter also never reports normal data queued before an urgent mark
  (reproduced in C with kqueue and with poll), which this patch neither causes nor cures;
  java/nio/channels/Selector/OutOfBand.java's later cases fail on it (known failure, R31).
Provenance: ours (ruling R26; the version in e820caf only ignored the event, which hid the spin
  inside select() rather than stopping it).
Exit: none while 10.9 is supported; harmless where the kernel never reports such an event.

